<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n10837" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">contĭōnābundus</span></b> , a,
		um, adj.
		contionor, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>proposing something</i> or <i>haranguing in a public assembly</i> (several times in Livy;
			elsewh. very rare). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>1.</b> In
			a bad sense; with <i>acc.</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">haec prope contionabundus circumibat homines,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Liv.
					3, 47, 3</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">5, 29,
			10</span></i>.— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b>
			In a good sense, <i><span class="en">Liv. 21, 53, 6</span></i>; <i><span class="en">40, 27, 8</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Tac. A. 1, 17
					<i>init.</i></span></i></div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
